      "writtenOn": "2022-03-03T22:50:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What do we need this for? It seems like signals should be initially deasserted, in which case a signal that should be electrically high at initialization should be marked as ACTIVE_LOW and I assume then initialization will set it high.",
      "revId": "f1410962bfd8429cdaa027db00eca1af5def3c5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6e324e59_109b82ab",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-04T00:04:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the named-gpios config, pins are able to specify an output state (e.g sys-reset has GPIO_ODR_HIGH), so to match this, the power signal config should be able to set an initial output value.\nThe initialisation will not set any initial value unless OUTPUT_INIT_{HIGH,LOW} is set, regardless of whether GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW is set (you just get the reset value, which physically low I guess).\nSo perhaps by default the init logic should set OUTPUT_INIT_LOW for all signals (for de-asserted at the start), and OUTPUT_INIT_HIGH if init-high is on, and let the ACTIVE_LOW be applied.\n\nFor pins like sys-rst, this would work.\nIt\u0027s possible that we may not need to set any pin as \u0027asserted\u0027 (OUTPUT_INIT_HIGH), but it\u0027s better to have this allowed in the config rather than relying code to explicitly have to set it (and risk glitches).\n\nPerhaps to be clearer, the config should be called \u0027init-assert\u0027, but the GPIO language consistently uses HIGH/LOW.",
      "parentUuid": "4567a1ae_26935254",
      "revId": "f1410962bfd8429cdaa027db00eca1af5def3c5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "74d364a1_608301c4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1361259
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-04T00:14:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "And how does named-gpios interact with the Zephyr configuration of each GPIO? I\u0027d expect the configuration for each IO to come from GPIO refs in the device tree rather than the shim\u0027s named-gpios, so why is the latter relevant?\n\nConcretely, we have something like this for a power signal:\n```\n\tpwr-en-pp3300-s5 {\n\t\tcompatible \u003d \"intel,ap-pwrseq-gpio\";\n\t\tdbg-label \u003d \"pwr-en-pp3300-s5\";\n\t\tenum-name \u003d \"PWR_EN_PP3300_A\";\n\t\tgpios \u003d \u003c\u0026gpiob 6 0\u003e;\n\t\toutput;\n\t};\n```\n\nSince this can specify an active-low flag for GPIOB6 if needed, why do we care about what might be in named-gpios or what the GPIO shim code is doing? It seems like it might be relevant if https://crrev.com/c/3492299 were to go in, but given the consensus on that is that we shouldn\u0027t, I don\u0027t see why this is useful.",
      "parentUuid": "6e324e59_109b82ab",
      "revId": "f1410962bfd8429cdaa027db00eca1af5def3c5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1ee8bd18_a094fdbf",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-03-04T03:24:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So the named-gpios is not relevant here except as reference for what is expected in the configuration.\nI expect if folks had to copy the config for legacy power sequence code, and use the named-gpios as reference, then the expectation is that the config will allow an initial state to be set for each GPIO.\n\nSo in the example you provide, this would be fine if the default init happens to be correct (the reset value of 0 on the pin)., but if this was an active low output, then the init at the moment would set to the physical state of 0, which is asserted.\nWith this change, an active low output would set it to the de-asserted value, , physical 1 on the pin. And if init-high is set, it would set it to physical 0, which matches what the config wants.\n\nSo the real fix here is that outputs should set to a known value at startup, with the default to set to de-asserted, but with the option to init it to asserted.",
      "parentUuid": "74d364a1_608301c4",
      "revId": "f1410962bfd8429cdaa027db00eca1af5def3c5b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
